[
    {
        "content": "<p>Currently, we have the following typeclass for splitting fractions:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsSplitFraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">split_add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Is there a use-case where the two sides of the split would be different? <br>\nI am wondering if we can simplify the typeclass to the following:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsHalfFraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">half</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">half_add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">half</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">half</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>cc <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"721721\">@Markus de Medeiros</span></p>",
        "id": 598302224,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Sammler",
        "timestamp": 1779967032
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also can this be merged into the main <code>Fraction</code> interface? (I don't really know what instantiations of this interface except for <code>Qp</code> and maybe reals would be and both of these should support a <code>half</code> operation. Are there other implementations for this interface planned?)</p>",
        "id": 598302489,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Sammler",
        "timestamp": 1779967129
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Does that simplify it?</p>",
        "id": 598304816,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1779967755
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I wrote it this way because I was trying to see what of the Qp arithmetic could be generalized. If <code>half</code> is easier then sure, but otherwise I don't see why we would specialize.</p>",
        "id": 598305710,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1779967933
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think half is easier because if you have a points to <code>l |-&gt;{q} v</code> and you split it twice, with half you get <code>l |-&gt;{q.half.half} v</code> 4 times, but with the current split you get four different points tos: <code>l |-&gt;{q.split.1.split.1} v</code>, <code>l |-&gt;{q.split.1.split.2} v</code>, <code>l |-&gt;{q.split.2.split.1} v</code> and <code>l |-&gt;{q.split.2.split.2} v</code> and you have to be very careful how you merge them back.<br>\nIf there is no use case for the generalized version, I think it is better to go with the more specialized version. Otherwise the unnecessary generalization just makes things more complex. We can always later generalize it when we actually need it.</p>",
        "id": 598306731,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Sammler",
        "timestamp": 1779968138
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>OK! Originally I was thinking there might be a way to make some variant of <a href=\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/krml225.pdf\">abstract read permissions</a> work, but we can just generalize this again later if so.</p>",
        "id": 598309170,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1779968854
    }
]